WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.460
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:09.719
Have a look and marvel at this learning-based
assembler robot that is able to put together

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:11.450
simple contraptions.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:15.549
Since this is a neural-network based learning
method, it needs to be trained to be able

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:16.650
to do this.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.850
So, how is it trained?

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:24.180
Normally, to train such an algorithm, we would
have to show it a lot of pairs of the same

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.910
contraption, and tell is that this is what
it looks like when it’s disassembled, and

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.220
what you see here is the same thing, assembled.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:35.340
If we did this, this method would be called
supervised learning.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:40.290
This would be very time consuming, and potentially
expensive as it would require the presence

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.650
of a human as well.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:47.200
A more convenient way would be to go for unsupervised
learning, where we just chuck a lot of things

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.930
on the table and say, “well, robot, you
figure it out”.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:57.070
However, this would be very inefficient, if
at all possible because we would have to provide

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:00.740
it many-many contraptions that wouldn’t
fit on the table.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:06.390
But this paper went for none of these solutions,
as they opted for a really smart self-supervised

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.390
technique.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:08.480
So what does that mean?

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.900
Well, first, we give the robot an assembled
contraption, and ask it to disassemble it.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:20.700
And therein lies the really cool idea, because
disassembling it is easier, and by rewinding

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.710
the process, it also gets to know how to assemble
it later.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:30.950
And, the training process takes place by assembling,
disassembling, and doing it over and over

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.840
again, several hundred times per object.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.440
Isn’t this amazing?

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.040
Love it.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.690
However, what is the point of all this?

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:44.159
Instead of all this, we could just add explicit
instructions to a non-learning based robot

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:45.930
to assemble the objects.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.720
Why not just do that?

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:53.479
And the answer lies in one of the most important
aspects within machine learning - generalization.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:59.000
If we program a robot to be able to assemble
one thing, it will be able to do exactly that

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.540
- assemble one thing.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:05.990
And whenever we have a new contraption on
our hands, we’ll need to reprogram it.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:10.590
However, with this technique, after the learning
process took place, we will be able to give

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:15.569
it a new, previously unseen object and it
will have a chance to assemble it.

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:17.650
This requires intelligence to perform.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:21.060
So, how good is it at generalization?

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:26.730
Well, get this, the paper reports that when
showing it new objects, it was able to successfully

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:32.270
assemble them 86% of the time.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:33.390
Incredible.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.540
So what about the limitations?

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.780
This technique works on a 2D planar surface,
for instance, this table, and while it is

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:45.950
able to insert most of these parts vertically
- it does not deal well with more complex

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:51.300
assemblies that require inserting screws and
pegs in a 45 degree angle.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:56.370
As we always say, two more papers down the
line, and this will likely be improved significantly.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:02.840
I you have ever bought a bed or a cupboard
and said, well, it just looks like a block,

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.900
how hard can it be to assemble?

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.690
Wait, does this thing have more than a 100
screws and pegs?

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:10.470
I wonder why?

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.940
And then, 4.5 hours later, you find out yourself.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:19.380
I hope techniques like these will help us
save time by enabling us to buy many of these

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:24.490
contraptions pre-assembled, and it can be
used for much, much more.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:26.370
What a time to be alive!

